description:
FILED s543t (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28, on the s543t tree = main 394a0012 + the batch) while probing #5465 (member 1, the context table: scratch/s543t/p/m1-break.pl in the s543t worktree).

REPRODUCER (perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    package It;
    use overload '<>' => sub { my $s = shift; wantarray ? ("L1", "L2") : ($s->{n}++ < 2 ? "it$s->{n}" : undef) };
    sub new { bless { n => 0 }, shift }
    package main;
    my $x = It->new;
    my $s = <$x>;  print "$s\n";      # perl it1
    my @l = <$x>;  print "@l\n";      # perl L1 L2   (the handler is called in LIST context)
    PCL: dies `Not a GLOB reference at ... line 6.` (exit 255) -- the runtime has no `<>` key at all (grep `"<>"` in cl/pcl-runtime.lisp: none); a class that also overloads `""` sees its `""` handler called instead (the readline of the stringified name).

WHERE: the readline entry (p-readline and its iterator twin) would ask p-find-overload for `<>` on a blessed operand before treating it as a handle.  NOTE for the fix: p-call-overload binds *wantarray* to scalar since s543t (#5465: every other key is G_SCALAR); `<>` is perl's ONE exception (pp.h tryAMAGICunTARGETlist, AMGf_want_list): in list context perl calls it in list context and spreads the list -- the dispatch must bind list context itself around its own call.
